============================================================
ATC System Design: CRC Cards
============================================================

Assumptions (General)
---------------------
- Incoming transponder data includes a unique hex-code identifier for each aircraft[cite: 21].
- The graphics refresh is managed by a system-level timer set to 10-second intervals[cite: 21].
- "Dangerous situations" are defined by minimum separation distance and altitude thresholds[cite: 21].

------------------------------------------------------------
CRC Card 1
------------------------------------------------------------
Class: SignalProcessor
Responsibilities:
- Listens for high-density broadcast packets from aircraft transponders[cite: 7, 12].
- Unpacks raw packet data into specific flight attributes like altitude and heading[cite: 7, 12].
- Transmits the parsed data to the central database for storage[cite: 7, 12].
Collaborators (if any):
- FlightDatabase[cite: 8, 20].
Assumptions (if any):
- Assumes the incoming data stream follows a standardized packet format[cite: 21].

------------------------------------------------------------
CRC Card 2
------------------------------------------------------------
Class: FlightDatabase
Responsibilities:
- Stores and indexes current data for all aircraft within the sector[cite: 7, 12].
- Updates existing records as new transponder packets arrive[cite: 7, 12].
- Provides specific flight details in response to controller queries[cite: 7, 12].
Collaborators (if any):
- (None) [cite: 8, 20].
Assumptions (if any):
- Assumes each aircraft is uniquely identified by its transponder ID[cite: 21].

------------------------------------------------------------
CRC Card 3
------------------------------------------------------------
Class: ConflictAnalyzer
Responsibilities:
- Scans the flight database to identify potential collisions or dangerous proximity[cite: 7, 12].
- Flags specific aircraft records that require immediate controller attention[cite: 7, 12].
- Signals safety alerts to be rendered on the display[cite: 7, 12].
Collaborators (if any):
- FlightDatabase[cite: 8, 20].
- RadarDisplay[cite: 8, 20].
Assumptions (if any):
- Assumes safety thresholds are updated dynamically based on airport traffic density[cite: 21].

------------------------------------------------------------
CRC Card 4
------------------------------------------------------------
Class: RadarDisplay
Responsibilities:
- Fetches the latest flight coordinates from the database every 10 seconds[cite: 7, 12].
- Renders a graphical representation of the airspace for the controller[cite: 7, 12].
- Highlights aircraft currently flagged by the conflict analyzer[cite: 7, 12].
Collaborators (if any):
- FlightDatabase[cite: 8, 20].
- ConflictAnalyzer[cite: 8, 20].
Assumptions (if any):
- Assumes the hardware can handle a complete visual refresh within the 10-second window[cite: 21].

------------------------------------------------------------
CRC Card 5
------------------------------------------------------------
Class: ControllerTerminal
Responsibilities:
- Captures user input for specific aircraft detail requests[cite: 7, 12].
- Queries the database for an aircraft's full flight history and data[cite: 7, 12].
- Displays the query results in an accessible format for the user[cite: 7, 12].
Collaborators (if any):
- FlightDatabase[cite: 8, 20].
Assumptions (if any):
- Assumes the controller interacts via a point-and-click graphical interface[cite: 21].

